# C3: the std submodules mlmf-hf-layout may name.
#
# This crate is bytes -> structure. Spec line 90 is explicit that it "never
# enumerates a directory", so there is no `fs`, no `path`, and no `io` here
# and there never should be -- the caller reads the file and hands over the
# bytes.
#
# `error` and `fmt` are present because `ShardError` implements
# `std::error::Error`, which requires `Display`, which is `std::fmt`. The
# first lets a consumer put it in a `Box<dyn Error>` alongside every other
# error in the workspace; the second is the trait that makes it printable.
#
# BOTH were added because the gate demanded them, not in advance -- and the
# plan predicted only `error`. `fmt` arrived from
# `purity.rs::every_gated_crate_performs_no_io` on the first full run:
#   "import names `std::fmt`, which is not on the permitted-std allow-list"
# which is the allow-list working as designed. An entry added "just in
# case" would be a permission nobody re-examines.
#
# The file exists rather than being absent because `purity.rs::allowed_std`
# panics when a gated member does not declare a list -- an absent file and
# an empty one would otherwise be indistinguishable, and the absent one
# would look like a crate nobody had considered.
error
fmt
